


By early afternoon, the night had become smaller on paper.




Not harmless. Not explained. Smaller.




Maren had six entries left in the unresolved column.




Jonah read them upside down from the other side of the desk.




“The cry.”




“Yes.”




“The impact in the shelter.”




“Yes.”




“The three movements around the stone.”




Maren left her own wording unchanged.




He looked up.




She did not correct him aloud.




He continued. “The sound after we got back to the trail. The voice-like sound. The last three hits.”




“Those are the six entries left on my sheet.”




“And you still think the episode should say nothing happened.”




Maren turned the monitor toward him.




“What are you writing?”




He read the rough release outline.




The first section laid out the corrected shelter history and the composite-map problem. The second documented the cry and the limitations on direction. The third showed the solo-control failure, including the frog call after her control statement. The fourth documented Jonah leaving the marked trail, Maren following, the unidentified stone, the phone abandonment, and the later recovery. The private townhouse footage was absent. So were the words grave, protector, attachment, warning, and sent us away except inside clearly attributed quotations that Maren had not yet decided to retain.




At the end she had typed:




UNATTENDED PHONE RECORDING: MULTIPLE ENVIRONMENTAL AND CONTACT SOUNDS. THREE LATE LOW IMPACTS REMAIN UNIDENTIFIED. NO VOICE OR SOURCE ESTABLISHED.




Jonah read it twice.




“You made the only clean part sound like a technical note.”




Maren did not change it.




“It is the part that happened after we left.”




She kept the cursor where it was.




Jonah looked at her.




“I’m not cutting it.”




He stood and walked to the shelf where Maren kept the envelope from the townhouse footage. The original card was inside, separate from the preserve material. He did not touch it.




“This is why that has to be in the episode,” he said.




Maren remained at the desk.




Jonah pointed at the envelope. “What happened to me didn’t start last night. You found me across the street with my hand cut open. I wrote pages I don’t remember writing. I disappeared from the car. Then we went back and I found the stone and the recorder kept running after we left. If you remove the beginning, the phone is just a weird audio file.”




Maren looked at the sealed envelope rather than at him.




“That footage shows you injured and disoriented before you had slept,” she said. “You asked me to keep it private.”




“I’m asking you now to use it.”




“At the house you asked me to stop. Later you asked me to use it. Those aren’t the same circumstances.”




“I know what I’m asking.”




Maren turned the chair toward him. “Then watch it first.”




His hand dropped from the shelf.




She took the envelope down and placed it on the desk between them without opening it.




“We agreed you would watch this before renewing consent,” she said. “We can do that now.”




Jonah stared at the envelope.




For several seconds the only sound in the room came from the computer fan.




“No,” he said.




Maren waited.




“I don’t want to watch it.”




“Then I’m not using it.”




He pressed his thumb against the edge of the fresh bandage on his palm.




“You won’t use the recording, but you won’t say nothing happened either,” he said.




Maren looked at the phone waveform still open on the monitor. “I’m not saying the last sounds were nothing.”




“But you won’t let them mean anything.”




“I’m not going to pretend we recorded what made them.”




Jonah nodded once, slowly.




Then he picked up the release folder from the end of the desk.




Their agreement allowed either of them to withdraw permission for personal footage before a first cut was approved. Jonah found the page, read the clause, and set it in front of her.




“If this is the version,” he said, “don’t use me.”




Maren did not reach for the pen.




He did.




He wrote the date beside his name and marked withdrawal of image and voice permission for the Harrow River episode. His left-handed signature leaned hard into the paper.




Maren read the line after he finished.




“All of it?” she asked.




“All of me.”




“The preserve footage, the shelter, the trail, the phone audio where you’re speaking?”




“Yes.”




“That means there may not be an episode.”




“I know.”




Maren signed as witness.




Jonah watched her put the form into the custody folder.




She returned the private-townhouse envelope to the shelf. Then she selected the entire release-outline document and moved it into a folder marked HOLD — CONSENT WITHDRAWN. She did not delete the raw media. She locked the phone copy and all cards under restricted project custody, with access limited to the two of them until they decided what could be retained for private documentation.




Jonah picked up his jacket.




At the door he stopped.




“You keep saying you’re protecting me,” he said.




Maren did not answer immediately.




He waited.




“I’m keeping the promise I made when you asked me to stop,” she said.




“I just gave you permission.”




“And you wouldn’t watch it first.”




His face tightened, but he did not argue.




Maren continued. “I believe you were frightened. I believe you heard movement. I believe we left the phone there and something made those last sounds after we were gone. I do not know what made them. I’m not going to use the worst night I’ve ever seen you have to tell people I know what caused those sounds.”




Jonah opened the door.




“I wish you had said that before we went back.”




“So do I.”




He left.




Maren stayed at the workstation until the sound of his car had passed the front windows.




The room was brighter than it had been when they started. Afternoon light reached across the floor and stopped beneath the equipment shelves. The Harrow River cards remained in their labeled sleeves. The phone lay faceup beside the keyboard, charging at sixty-one percent.




She closed the release outline.




Then she opened the unattended recording again.




Not the enhanced copy. Not a filtered export. Just the raw duplicate she had transferred that morning.




She moved the playhead to the point where their last audible footfalls ended and listened from there without headphones.




Insects. Distant road noise. A frog below the rise. Leaves shifting in several short runs. The low contact Jonah had pointed out. The quieter scrape. Wind in higher branches. Long intervals with nothing that required a name.




Maren kept her hands off the keyboard.




Near the end, the first of the three contacts sounded.




Low. Brief.




The second came after several seconds, stronger on the recording than the first.




Then the third.




The phone case rattled once against the stone.




Twelve seconds of ordinary night followed.




The file ended.